# Software Testing and Quality Assurance: Breaking Software on Purpose

> Testing is the art of finding the defects a developer hopes are not
> there, before the user does. It cannot prove software correct — only
> the presence of bugs, never their absence — but a disciplined blend
> of levels, techniques and automation turns quality from a wish into
> a measurable, managed property.

## 1. Testing, QA, QC — Three Different Things

[definition]
**Software testing** is the process of executing software to find
defects. **Quality Control (QC)** is the broader inspection activity
(design reviews, code walkthroughs, testing) that verifies work
products against requirements. **Quality Assurance (QA)** is
process-focused: defining and improving the procedures that prevent
defects from being created in the first place.
[/definition]

| Term | Focus | Question asked |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Testing | product | does this build fail anywhere? |
| QC | product & documents | do work products meet specs? |
| QA | process | are we working in a way that produces quality? |

[important]
Testing shows **presence** of defects, not their absence
(Dijkstra). Exhaustive testing of every input combination is
impossible — a program with three 32-bit inputs has more cases than
seconds in a billion years — so every test strategy is really the art
of choosing a *representative, high-yield* sample.
[/important]

## 2. The Test Levels

| Level | Scope | Typical target |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Unit** | a single module in isolation | a function, a class |
| **Integration** | interactions between modules | interfaces, data flow |
| **System** | the whole system against requirements | end-to-end behaviour |
| **Acceptance (UAT)** | business readiness, by/for the user | release decision |

Integration proceeds **bottom-up** (test lower modules first, drive
with drivers), **top-down** (higher modules first, stub the missing
lower ones) or **big-bang** (everything at once — cheap, but defects
are hardest to localise). **Sandwich** mixes both directions.

## 3. Black-Box Techniques

Black-box tests derive cases from specifications, without seeing code.

- **Equivalence partitioning:** split inputs into classes where all
  members should behave identically; one representative per class. A
  field accepting ages 18–59 gives three partitions: below 18, 18–59,
  above 59.
- **Boundary value analysis (BVA):** defects cluster at edges — test
  17, 18, 19 and 58, 59, 60 rather than the middles (traditional 5-
  or 7-value BVA around each boundary).
- **Decision tables:** map combinations of conditions to expected
  actions (discount rules, admission rules).
- **State transition testing:** exercise every legal (and an illegal)
  transition — login → locked after three failures.

**Worked example.** "A discount of 10% applies for bills over ₹1,000
but under ₹5,000; 15% above ₹5,000." Partitions: ≤1000, 1001–5000,
>5000. Boundary values: 1000, 1001, 5000, 5001 — four cases that
  catch most of the off-by-one bugs this spec can contain.

## 4. White-Box Techniques

White-box tests derive cases from the code's structure.

[definition]
**Statement coverage** requires every executable statement to run at
least once. **Branch (decision) coverage** requires every branch
outcome (true AND false of each condition) to be exercised — always
at least as strong as statement coverage. **Path coverage** requires
every possible route through the code — usually impossible for
programs with loops.
[/definition]

Cyclomatic complexity $V(G) = E - N + 2P$ (edges − nodes + 2 ×
components) counts the linearly independent paths; a practical rule is
that $V(G) \le 10$ keeps a module testable, and $V(G)$ equals the
minimum number of test cases needed for branch coverage.

**Worked example.** A module with two sequential `if` statements has
4 paths and $V(G) = 3$; two `if-else` branches in sequence typically
need 4 test cases for full branch coverage — one per truth
combination of the decisions.

## 5. Test Types and the Testing Pyramid

- **Functional:** does the feature do what it should?
- **Non-functional:** performance/load (response under expected and
  peak traffic), stress (beyond limits), security, usability,
  reliability, portability.
- **Regression:** after every change, re-run the suite to confirm old
  features still work — the safety net that makes refactoring
  possible.
- **Smoke/sanity:** quick checks deciding whether a build is worth
  deeper testing.
- **Alpha (in-house) vs beta (selected real users)** — pre-release
  exposure stages.

The testing **pyramid** recommends many fast unit tests, fewer
integration tests, and a thin top of slow, flaky end-to-end UI
tests — inverted pyramids (all UI, no unit) are slow, brittle and
expensive.

## 6. Defects, Severity and Priority

A **defect** is a mismatch between expected and actual behaviour; a
**failure** is what the user observes when a defect executes. Triage
assigns two independent axes:

| | High priority | Low priority |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **High severity** | crash on checkout — fix now | crash in a screen shipped next quarter |
| **Low severity** | logo typo on launch day | cosmetic bug in a rare screen |

Severity is technical impact; priority is business urgency. A
misspelled brand name on the homepage is low severity, high priority.

## 7. Debugging vs Testing, and Automation

Testing finds that a defect exists; **debugging** (breakpoints,
logging, bisection, rubber-duck reasoning) locates and removes its
cause. Regression suites, load tests and CI pipelines are automated
because they are repetitive; exploratory testing stays human because
it depends on curiosity and pattern recognition. Modern practice
wires the suite into **continuous integration**: every commit runs
the tests, and a red build blocks the merge — quality stops being a
final phase and becomes a property of every change.

## 8. Summary

Test at every level (unit → integration → system → acceptance),
choose cases with black-box heuristics (partitions, boundaries) and
white-box structure (branches, complexity), never confuse severity
with priority, and automate the regression net. The goal is not to
prove the software perfect — impossible — but to reduce the risk of
release to a level the business consciously accepts.
